  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong high pressure building into the Mid-Atlantic \nand low pressure well off the FL coast will result in a period \nof continued long period swell across the OBX. The high waves \ncombined with King Tides this week will lead to additional minor\ncoastal flooding impacts.\n\nFor Hatteras Island, seas offshore are in the 4-5 foot range \nearly this afternoon and continuing to subside, but the long \nperiod swells will continue to cause issues for vulnerable \nareas. For ocean- side: high tide is around 0900 PM EDT today. \nSoundside high tides lag by up to 3 hours behind the oceanside \ntides. The National Parks Service and NCDOT have reported \nsubstantial beach erosion from south Nags Head to Buxton, along \nwith significant overwash occurring on side streets in Rodanthe.\nDue to hazardous road conditions and flooding, NC-12 remains \nclosed from the Marc Basnight Bridge to Rodanthe. \n\nMinor coastal flooding of vulnerable locations will be likely \nwell into this week despite the diminishing of winds, with \n\"sunny day\" flooding across much of the tidal regions of ENC \naround times of high tide, then slowly improve as the King Tides\nease later in the week and into the weekend. Coastal flood \nadvisories continue through the high tide cycle on Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry high pres with near climo temps expected\ntoday and tonight, then warming to above normal mid week through\nthe weekend. A coastal trough offshore could bring isolated\nshowers to coastal communities tomorrow into tomorrow night.\nPoPs have been increased to ~20% to account for this. Thunder\nchances remain below 5% along the coast tomorrow. Late week \ninto the weekend, hts/thicknesses build further, and temps \nclimbing back to 5-10 degrees above climo with highs around 80F \ninland to low 70s OBX. Low temps will warm above climo as well \nwith readings in the mid 50s for most locales away from the \nimmediate coast. Next chance of rain is Sunday.",
